/** \page dla_bddc PETSc BDDC preconditioning

Select PETSc as the solver backend and set these MORIS parameters:

\code{.cpp}
// Solver warehouse parameter list:
tWarehouse.set( "SOL_TPL_Type", static_cast< uint >( sol::MapType::Petsc ) );
tWarehouse.set( "SOL_PETSc_Mat_Type", "is" );

// PETSc preconditioner parameter list, referenced by the linear algorithm:
tPreconditioner.set( "PCType", "bddc" );

// PETSc linear algorithm parameter list:
tLinearAlgorithm.set( "KSPType", "fgmres" );
\endcode

SOL_PETSc_Mat_Type defaults to "aij". Existing AIJ assembly and preconditioners
remain available. BDDC requires "is" (MATIS), selected before matrix construction;
selecting BDDC with AIJ reports an error. Direct Matrix_PETSc construction also
accepts "is" as its third argument. PETSc's -mat_type is option overrides the
constructor selection, but is global and can affect auxiliary matrices; prefer
the warehouse parameter for production runs.

Each MPI rank supplies its local element/integration contributions once. Its
owned-and-shared DOF map must contain all DOFs in those contributions. Shared
DOFs have the same PETSc global ID on all ranks, while the local MATIS matrix
includes both owned and shared DOFs. Ghost elements must not duplicate physical
contributions. MATIS uses the same global vector ownership as AIJ. If the overlap
map contains ghost DOFs without local contributions, BDDC uses a compressed
preconditioning copy; the original MATIS map is retained for reassembly.

The implementation supports both element-by-element graph construction and
owned-row sparsity preallocation through MatISSetPreallocation. The latter
scatters the global row counts to the subdomains and may overallocate. Repeated
assembly zeros the existing local matrix values and preserves the graph.

Strongly constrained DOFs retained in the global system receive unit rows and
columns; their Newton-correction RHS must be zero. The constraint flags are
communicated to all subdomains, and Dirichlet indices are supplied to BDDC.
Nonzero essential data must already be treated consistently in the residual.
Weak boundary terms continue to be assembled as element contributions.

BDDC tuning belongs to the PETSc preconditioner parameter list:

\code{.cpp}
tPreconditioner.set( "use_bddc_defaults", false );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_symmetric", true );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_detect_disconnected", true );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_use_vertices", true );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_use_edges", true );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_use_faces", true );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_use_stiffness_scaling", true );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_dirichlet_solver", "mumps" );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_neumann_solver", "mumps" );
// Select a parallel coarse factor package only for a nonempty coarse problem.
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_coarse_solver", "mumps" );
\endcode

use_bddc_defaults defaults to true, preserving native PETSc switches for existing
inputs. Set it to false to apply managed boolean switches, including the deluxe/sub-Schur
settings below. Empty
factor-package strings retain PETSc defaults regardless of use_bddc_defaults.
A nonempty bddc_coarse_solver also selects LU for the coarse problem; leave it
empty for the serial example's potentially empty coarse problem. PETSc must have
been built with any requested factor package. For a general direct AIJ solve,
use PCType = "lu" and factor_solver_type = "mumps" (or another PETSc factor package).
The legacy PCType = "mumps" path remains available with its existing behavior.

These options are translated in cl_DLA_Preconditioner_PETSc.cpp because PETSc
exposes several switches only through its options parser. The linear solver
pushes a temporary options database during KSPSetFromOptions and KSPSetUp, so
nested Dirichlet, Neumann, and coarse solvers receive the settings without
leaking them to other MORIS solvers. Prefixes are honored, and explicit native
PETSc options override managed parameter values. For nonsymmetric problems set
bddc_symmetric to false with use_bddc_defaults = false. Native -pc_bddc_* options
remain available as expert overrides.

For strongly varying interface stiffness (material jumps, cut cells, or ghost
penalties), start with bddc_use_stiffness_scaling = true. Deluxe scaling instead
uses local interface Schur complements. PETSc's sub-Schur symmetry and positive-
definiteness assumptions are independent of bddc_symmetric. For a nonsymmetric
coupled problem such as the thermoelastic pressure vessel, use:

\code{.cpp}
tPreconditioner.set( "use_bddc_defaults", false );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_symmetric", false );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_use_deluxe_scaling", true );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_sub_schurs_symmetric", false );
tPreconditioner.set( "bddc_sub_schurs_posdef", false );
\endcode

The new defaults are deluxe off and both sub-Schur assumptions true, matching
PETSc. Their native option names are -pc_bddc_use_deluxe_scaling,
-sub_schurs_symmetric, and -sub_schurs_posdef, all preceded by the PC prefix.
Deluxe weights supersede diagonal stiffness weights when enabled. The pressure-
vessel parallel tests use MUMPS for robust pivoting; simply enabling deluxe with
its SPD assumptions caused a zero-pivot Cholesky failure in PETSc 3.23.2.
Adaptive spectral constraints documented by PETSc require an SPD problem; do not
assume that recipe applies to a full nonsymmetric thermoelastic operator.

KSPErrorIfNotConverged, KSPMonitorTrueResidual, and KSPConvergedReason belong to
the PETSc linear algorithm list and default to false for backward compatibility.
SOL_PETSc_Log_File belongs to the solver warehouse; a nonempty filename starts
PETSc logging and writes its event table at finalization. SOL_PETSc_Options is
an optional warehouse string for expert overrides; ordinary BDDC inputs should
use the named preconditioner and linear-algorithm parameters.

The implementation uses PETSc's default interface constraints. Set the PETSc
preconditioner parameter bddc_use_dof_splitting to true to construct separate
constraints for each requested physical DOF type, using MORIS's owned DOF maps.
This is useful for coupled problems with multiple displacement/temperature fields.
For Cartesian nodal FEM subdomains, bddc_use_subdomain_corners optionally
supplements graph vertices with geometric corners obtained from MTK/MSI metadata.
It requires one active unit-weight coefficient at each corner and is disabled by
default. See projects/EXA/structure/linear/bddc for HMR/FEM examples and tests.

Rigid-body modes and other custom primal constraints are not derived automatically;
these may be needed for robust elasticity, enriched, or high-contrast problems. Disconnected subdomains may require
-pc_bddc_detect_disconnected.

Regression test: run DLA-test with the Catch filter [PETSc_BDDC] using 1, 2, and
4 MPI ranks. It compares AIJ/MATIS actions with an analytic assembled operator,
checks column-major nonsymmetric element input, and solves with BDDC after
repeated assembly, with and without retained Dirichlet DOFs.

PETSc references:
https://petsc.org/release/manualpages/Mat/MATIS/
https://petsc.org/release/manualpages/Mat/MatISSetPreallocation/
https://petsc.org/release/manualpages/PC/PCBDDC/
https://github.com/petsc/petsc/blob/v3.23.2/src/ksp/pc/impls/bddc/bddcschurs.c
*/
